# The dataset is byte-compared, so git must not rewrite it on the way out.
#
# `build_dataset.py --check` regenerates every file into memory and compares
# BYTES against what is on disk. That is deliberate: comparing parsed JSON would
# let a whitespace or encoding change through, and this data is evidence.
#
# This repository has no root `.gitattributes` and `core.autocrlf` is true on the
# machine it is developed on, so without the line below git converts LF to CRLF
# on checkout. MEASURED: a plain `git checkout` of `manifest.json` added 11 CR
# bytes and `--check` then exited 1 on a dataset that had not drifted at all --
# a false alarm on every fresh Windows clone, which would teach the one gate
# this directory has to be ignored.
#
# `-text` means "never convert, in either direction". The bytes in the commit are
# the bytes in the working tree on every platform, which is the only thing that
# makes the check meaningful.
* -text
